export abstract anchor FactoryResources:
    buildAndTestCapacity:
        The factory manages build and test capacity in addition to agent
        concurrency. Ordinary code queues build and test jobs and enforces
        available capacity; AI agents do not make routine capacity allocation
        decisions. Remote builds are a first-class capability required in the
        initial version. The resource model remains unspecified.
    remoteBuildWiring:
        Remote builds have deterministic controls and behavior implemented in
        code, including queueing, dispatch, and capacity enforcement. Agents
        handle the project- and machine-specific wiring that connects those
        controls to actual remote build environments. Preserve this separation
        between deterministic controls and agent-based wiring. The wiring's
        representation remains unspecified; its reuse, repair, and validation
        follow the general integration principle and wiring validation policy.
    spendingLimits:
        API account budgets established during setup are enforced in the
        initial scope. This supersedes the earlier deferral of custom spending
        caps insofar as API account budgets are concerned. Additional per-order
        or factory-wide caps remain on the roadmap. Usage limits also account
        for available spending in an API account and the remaining usage
        allowance on a subscription account.
        Usage and availability should be obtained from the existing harnesses
        or provider APIs as much as possible. When an account runs out of
        available usage, the factory asks the engineer how to proceed.
        Independent work continues where possible under the engineer-input
        policy. When fresh usage readings are unavailable, continue assigning
        work using cautious estimates based on the last known allowance and
        subsequent work. Missing readings alone do not stop dispatch. Estimates
        remain subject to the account limits and budgets; the exact estimation
        method and uncertainty handling remain unresolved.
    resourceOptimization:
        The factory aims for speed, quality, and economy. Initial setup asks
        the engineer how many Claude subscriptions are available, their kinds,
        and the budgets available for API accounts, along with the relevant
        account resources. Maximize use of available subscription allowances
        without exceeding them, and keep API spending within its budgets.
        When appropriate, ask the engineer whether more API funds can be added;
        do not assume that additional funds are authorized.
        Ordinary code continually adjusts resource usage through a PID-style
        feedback algorithm. The target is maximum useful throughput at all
        times within all applicable constraints, without exceeding subscription
        allowances, API budgets, or build capacity. Adapt allocation to the
        current bottleneck. When subscription availability is the constraint,
        maximize effective work within that allowance. When build servers are
        the constraint, maximize useful subscription work in planning at a
        pace aligned with build throughput. Optimize effectiveness rather than
        utilization of an individual resource in isolation.
        The exact measurements, control outputs, tuning, and treatment of
        delayed usage data remain unresolved. A downstream-work priority rule
        has not been adopted as the scheduling policy.
    adaptiveModelSelection:
        Model routing depends on thresholds set by the engineer. The factory
        continually researches the changing model landscape and adapts its
        optimization within those thresholds. Evaluate models using returned
        work and higher-tier reviewers' assessments, alongside capability,
        speed, throttling, and API or subscription economics. Model selection
        must evolve with observed results rather than rely on fixed rankings.
        The engineer currently describes DeepSeek Flash as very fast with
        generous throttling, and MiMo as more capable but slower, aggressively
        throttled, and offering a subscription plan. These are the engineer's
        observations, not permanent provider guarantees or verified current
        product claims. The factory asks the engineer before trying a newly
        researched model. Exact thresholds and evaluation methods remain
        unresolved.
    accountAllocation:
        Automatically distribute new work across approved accounts according
        to the best optimization of speed, quality, and economy within the
        engineer's configured thresholds, account allowances, and API budgets.
        Routine allocation among approved accounts does not require approval
        for each switch. Exhausted-account notifications and requests for
        additional funds still follow the engineer-input policies.
    paidFallbackDecision:
        When subscription capacity is temporarily throttled and paid API
        capacity could avoid the wait, ask the engineer to choose between
        spending more to finish sooner and waiting to save money. Available
        API budget alone does not authorize this tradeoff. Routine allocation
        within an already approved policy remains automatic.
    taskSpecificModelEvaluation:
        Learn model strengths and weaknesses separately by kind of work using
        returned results and higher-tier review assessments. Use that history
        to guide routing of similar work. A model's success on routine changes
        must not imply equal suitability for complex numerical algorithms or
        other different kinds of work. Task categories and the exact evaluation
        method remain unresolved.
    outcomeEfficiency:
        Judge model efficiency by the total time and cost to reach an accepted
        result, including review passes and corrections, rather than only the
        first attempt. Smarter models perform a review pass where appropriate
        to assess the result. Use these assessments in the task-specific model
        evaluation, while retaining risk-appropriate review depth.
